{
  "raw": {
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/City-Campus-Services-Officer_JR0000017949": {
      "title": "City Campus Services Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\n·        Two full time positions available ·        1 x continuing position ·        1 x Fixed-term position until 31 December 2027 ·        Both positions commence 4 January 2027 ·        Higher Education Officer 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 p.a.  ·        Location: City Campus / Kaurna Country ·        View Position Description   About the Position   Our team of Customer Service Officers, (internally referred to as City Campus Services Officers) are responsible for providing customer-focused, front-line support services to students, staff and a wide range of stakeholders. The City Campus Services team embodies the values and ambitions of Flinders University by putting students first, acting with integrity and courage, embracing innovation and pursuing excellence, while working collaboratively to create meaningful impact for our students, partners and communities. Combining exceptional solution-focused customer service with a broad knowledge relating to Flinders University and the City Campus, our City Campus Services Officers are required to work across service hours of 8am to 8pm during peak periods and 8am to 6pm during off-peak periods . Please note, this is a frontline service position and flexibility may be limited. This role provides a fantastic opportunity to learn and develop on the job, expand your problem-solving and organisational skills, while working collaboratively within the team.   Some key responsibilities include: Assisting new and continuing students by providing transactional services and informed advice on enrolment matters. Assist future students with course options, entry requirements, fees, and admission processes, highlighting Flinders City Campus as a destination of choice. Connecting students and campus visitors to the appropriate contacts and resources within the University. Creating and updating documentation for new and established procedures within City Campus to drive consistency, efficiency, and accountability in all facets of front-line customer support.   This recruitment process will fill two City Campus Services Officer positions: one continuing and one fixed-term until 31 December 2027, both commencing 4 January 2027. Applicants will be considered for both opportunities through a single recruitment process.   About You     As a Customer Services Officer, you will have experience in a high-volume, fast paced, demanding front-line customer service environment. You will be a reliable, supportive and adaptable team player who is passionate about assisting others and providing an exceptional customer experience. You will have excellent interpersonal and communication skills, allowing you to provide engaging support, warm referrals and create positive outcomes in a fast-paced environment. You will actively seek to improve processes, systems and procedures to elevate the overall customer experience. About Flinders City Campus They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Located at the heart of Adelaide’s most vibrant and connected cultural hub, Flinders City Campus at Festival Plaza is a collaborative, state-of-the-art campus delivering the optimal learning experience. Our campus provides greater connection for education and research partnerships and innovative curriculum opportunities that extend far beyond traditional study.   We are passionate about collaborating with staff and students across the University to develop, improve, and implement communication, orientation, engagement, and retention activities that help students achieve success at Flinders. Our mission is to help students feel welcomed, supported, and empowered with confidence to succeed at University. To learn more about why Flinders is a great place to work, please click here.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.    So, why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options  Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers   We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit Suitability Statement of no more than 2 pages, addressing the below four Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Experience in a high-volume, fast paced, demanding front-line customer service environment where a positive, engaged and solution-focused approach is required. High level of customer service skills, including written and verbal interpersonal skills and the ability to develop, maintain and enhance relationships with customers. Ability to contribute to a positive team culture and to develop and foster networks with staff across the University. Effective to well-developed organisational, time and workload management skills, including ability to prioritise workload to meet strict deadlines. For further instructions on how to complete a Suitability Statement, please click here. For more information regarding this position, we strongly encourage you to contact Thu Trotter. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 15 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Flinders-Rural-Health-SA---Victor-Harbor/Rural-Clinical-Educator---Renmark---Victor-Harbor_JR0000017945": {
      "title": "Rural Clinical Educator – Renmark / Victor Harbor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Part-time (0.1 FTE and 0.2 FTE position available) | Fixed term (2 years) Academic Level C | $141,370 - $162,619 (pro rata) Location: Renmark - River Murray & Mallee Region Location: Victor Harbor - Ramindjeri View Position Description - Renmark – 0.1 FTE View Position Description - Victor Harbor – 0.2 FTE   About the Role   An exciting opportunity is available for an experienced rural clinician to join the SA Rural Medical Program as a Rural Clinical Educator. Based in either Renmark or Victor Harbor, you will share your expertise through small-group teaching and mentoring, helping medical students learn within authentic rural healthcare and community settings. This role is ideally suited to clinicians who are passionate about supporting the next generation of doctors while remaining connected to their local region and health service community.   As a Rural Clinical Educator, you will be responsible for: Delivering engaging clinical teaching, clinical skills training and simulation-based education to Doctor of Medicine students. Facilitating small-group learning experiences within authentic rural healthcare and community settings. Providing mentoring, career guidance and academic support to students undertaking rural medical training. Supporting clinical supervisors and strengthening partnerships across local healthcare services and communities.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to \"Changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters\", by 2035 through delivering high-quality, student-centred medical education in authentic rural healthcare settings. By supporting, mentoring and inspiring future doctors through personalised small-group learning experiences, the role advances Flinders' commitment to putting students at the centre of everything we do and helping them achieve their full potential.   About You   You are an experienced clinician who is passionate about rural healthcare and developing the next generation of medical professionals. Whether you are an established medical educator or looking to transition into academia, you enjoy sharing your expertise through teaching, mentorship and clinical leadership while remaining connected to your local community.   To succeed in this role, you will bring: Contemporary clinical experience and a strong understanding of rural healthcare practice. A passion for mentoring and supporting learners through small-group, student-centred education. Excellent relationship-building skills and the ability to engage with students, clinicians and healthcare partners. A commitment to advancing rural health outcomes and contributing to the future rural medical workforce.   Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA   Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA (FRRHSA) facilitates high-quality multidisciplinary health education and research in our State's regional areas. We have a strong national and international reputation for providing community-engaged inter-professional training for medical, nursing, and allied health students. This includes the SA Rural Medical (SARM) program and multidisciplinary rural clinical placements. Through this practice, we bring new and exciting opportunities to actively engage and work with rural communities.    Life at Flinders   They say know your place. We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. It is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA).   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Please make it clear on your application whether you are applying for the Renmark or Victor Harbor position. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact James Padley Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 16 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Gove-District-Hospital-Nhulunbuy--Yolngu-Country/Remote-Student-Services-Officer_JR0000017942": {
      "title": "Remote Student Services Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-02\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time (36.75 hours per week) | Fixed term until 30 November 2027 Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 Location: Nhulunbuy – Yolngu View Position Description   About the Role As the Remote Student Services Officer, you will play a vital role in supporting students undertaking placements and learning experiences across rural and remote health settings. Based in Nhulunbuy and working within a small, collaborative team, you will build strong relationships with students, healthcare providers and community stakeholders to enhance the rural student experience. You will also be responsible for: Coordinating administrative support for remote student placements and teaching activities. Delivering high-quality customer service and support across the student lifecycle. Building strong partnerships with healthcare providers, academic staff and community stakeholders. Planning events, preparing reports and contributing to initiatives that enhance student engagement and rural workforce outcomes. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to “Changing 10 Million Lives, Every One Matters” by 2035 through supporting students to succeed in rural and remote learning environments. By delivering responsive support, fostering positive placement experiences, and connecting students with key stakeholders, the role contributes to Flinders’ Student Centric strategic driver, ensuring students are at the heart of everything we do. About You You are a highly organised and customer-focused professional with a passion for supporting students and contributing to positive educational outcomes. You thrive in a collaborative environment, enjoy building relationships with diverse stakeholders, and can manage competing priorities while delivering exceptional service.   To succeed in this role, you will bring: Experience in administration, student services, education support or a related field. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. Excellent organisational skills and the ability to coordinate multiple activities and priorities simultaneously. A proactive, adaptable approach and a commitment to delivering high-quality service and continuous improvement. Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT   At Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT , our aim is to be a hub for innovative health research, education, and workforce development across the Northern Territory.  We engage with students, community, and stakeholders to provide critical programs, such as the Northern Territory Medical Program, Paramedicine, Rural Clinical School, and the University Department of Rural Health.   Our postgraduate education pathways (Remote Health Practice program), provides workforce development through Indigenous health and clinical skills training, and we have a community driven research program led by researchers committed to improving health outcomes. We work alongside Poche SA+NT focussing on Indigenous leadership capacity development and developing the Indigenous health workforce.  All activities are driven by our values which focus on the long-term and short-term needs of the NT community.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place. We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are required to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the NT Health policy. Northern Territory Government legislation (the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007) now requires all individuals in child related employment or whose duties involve, or are likely to involve, contact with a child, to hold a valid 'Working with Children Clearance' (Ochre Card). This position involves child related work and is assessed as requiring a Working with Children Clearance/Ochre Card.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Gill Paynter Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 16 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Associate-Lecturer---Lecturer-in-Social-Work_JR0000017929": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Social Work",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nMultiple opportunities available across full-time and part-time appointments Continuing and fixed-term positions offering flexibility to suit a range of career stages Academic Level A | $90,930 - $110,197 Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 (pro rata for part-time) Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Associate Lecturer Position Description View Lecturer (Teaching Specialist (Continuing)) Position Description View Lecturer (Teaching Specialist (Fixed – Part-time)) Position Description View Lecturer (Teaching & Research (Continuing)) Position Description View Lecturer (Teaching & Research (Fixed – Full-time)) Position Description About the Associate Lecturer (Level A) Role As an Associate Lecturer in Social Work, you will play a key role in delivering high-quality, student-centred learning experiences that support the development of future social work professionals. Working within a collaborative academic environment, you will contribute to the design, delivery and continuous improvement of Social Work topics, applying innovative teaching approaches and technologies to enhance student engagement and success. This role offers the opportunity to make a meaningful impact through teaching, curriculum development, student support and professional engagement. You will: Deliver engaging learning experiences through lectures, tutorials, workshops, practical classes and student assessment across Social Work topics. Contribute to curriculum development, topic planning and the ongoing enhancement of teaching practices, learning resources and educational technologies. Provide academic guidance, pastoral support and professional advice to students to foster their academic success and career development. Engage in service and professional activities, including participation in committees, community engagement initiatives and ongoing professional development within the discipline.   About the Lecturer (Level B) Roles We are seeking passionate and dedicated academics to join the Social Work discipline in either a Teaching Specialist or Teaching and Research capacity. These positions play a key role in delivering high-quality education that prepares students for professional social work practice while contributing to a collaborative and student-centred learning environment. Depending on the appointment type, the successful candidate will focus primarily on excellence in teaching and field education or combine high-quality teaching with the development of an active research profile that advances knowledge and practice within the discipline. You will: Deliver engaging and contemporary teaching that supports student learning, professional capability development, and graduate success. Contribute to curriculum development, topic coordination, assessment design, and continuous quality improvement activities. Foster strong relationships with students, field educators, community organisations, industry partners, and professional bodies. Contribute to discipline, College, and University initiatives, including accreditation, community engagement, and, for Teaching and Research appointments, scholarly and research activities that enhance the impact of the discipline.   These positions make a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan, Changing Lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 by working towards strengthening our ‘Here for Good’ core enabler through the preparation of future social work professionals who improve outcomes for individuals, families, and communities.   About You You are an enthusiastic and collaborative Social Work academic with a passion for student learning and professional practice. You will bring contemporary disciplinary expertise, strong communication skills, and a commitment to fostering inclusive learning environments. Whether your strengths lie in teaching excellence or the integration of teaching and research, you will be motivated to contribute to student success, academic quality, and positive community impact. You will demonstrate: Expertise in Social Work, with qualifications and professional experience that support high-quality teaching and student learning. Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships across students, colleagues, industry, and community partners. A commitment to inclusive, student-centred education and continuous improvement in teaching practice. The ability to contribute to scholarly, research, professional, and service activities that enhance the discipline and broader community impact.   College of Human Sciences and Culture The College of Human Sciences and Culture brings together expertise across education, psychology, social work, humanities, arts, and social sciences to advance human understanding and address the complex challenges facing individuals, communities, and societies. Through world-class teaching, impactful research, and interdisciplinary collaboration, we inspire new ways of thinking, foster human development and cultural understanding, and empower graduates to create meaningful change in a rapidly evolving world. Life at Flinders They say know your place. We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. It is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the relevant position description. Associate Lecturer Position Description Teaching Specialist (Continuing) Position Description Teaching Specialist (Fixed – Part-time) Position Description Teaching & Research (Continuing) Position Description Teaching & Research (Fixed – Full-time) Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Kathomi Gatwiri or Damien Riggs . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 15 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
