    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Flinders-Medical-Centre--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Lecturer-in-Physiology_JR0000017922": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Physiology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Fixed term until 31 December 2028 Part-time at 0.5 FTE ( We are open to this 0.5 FTE being worked across multiple days) Academic Level C | $141,370 - $162,619 (pro rata) Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   About the Role An exciting opportunity has become available to lead physiology education and influence the learning experiences of future health professionals. As the Senior Lecturer in Physiology, you will provide academic leadership across physiology education, driving excellence in teaching, curriculum innovation and student success within the College of Medicine and Public Health. This role offers the opportunity to shape future health professionals through contemporary, student-centred learning experiences while contributing to strategic educational initiatives.   You will be responsible for: Leading the design, delivery and continuous improvement of physiology teaching across a range of programs and study modes. Coordinating topics and courses, while providing academic guidance, mentoring and support to students. Driving curriculum innovation through contemporary teaching practices, learning technologies and educational leadership. Contributing to accreditation, compliance, partnerships and broader College, University and professional service activities. For additional information on the Key Position Responsibilities, please view the position description .   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to \"Changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters\" by 2035 through delivering outstanding physiology education that prepares future health professionals to improve health outcomes in the communities they serve. The role reflects the University’s Student Centric values by creating engaging, inclusive and high-quality learning experiences that support student success and achievement. About You You are an accomplished educator and academic leader with a passion for delivering outstanding student learning outcomes. Combining discipline expertise with innovative teaching approaches, you are committed to fostering engaging learning environments and contributing to the advancement of health professions education.   To be successful in this role, you will bring: A PhD, EdD or equivalent qualification, together with significant tertiary teaching experience. A demonstrated record of excellence in teaching, curriculum development and scholarship of learning and teaching. Experience using innovative and online learning approaches to enhance student engagement and success. Outstanding communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work effectively across academic, student and industry settings.   College of Medicine and Public Health               At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place. We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Nick Spencer, Matthew Flinders Professor at nicholas.spencer@flinders.edu.au Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 15 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Team-Leader---Senior-Technical-Officer_JR0000017921": {
      "title": "Team Leader - Senior Technical Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-30\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Continuing Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role With significant clinical experience, the Senior Technical Officer will collaborate with the Senior Manager to distribute team workloads and coordinate the day-to-day activities in Flinders clinical skills and simulation laboratories. The incumbent will provide services in a range of technical functions, including simulation activities, to support the programs of the College of Health and Enablement. The incumbent will be responsible for ensuring workshop preparation and associated simulation equipment supports the learning requirements of students working in the Healthcare Simulation Laboratories. The is also responsible for: Managing and leading a team of service-oriented professionals, including planning and allocating staff resources, developing, coaching and mentoring employees and managing performance to deliver high-quality services. Organise the reallocation of staff duties and workloads in the event of staff absence. Provide technical advice and assistance during practical classes including demonstration of simulation equipment, procedures and techniques as required and to ensure safe and effective operations. Assume the functional responsibilities of other Team Leaders and Senior Manager in their absence. Quality control of clinical laboratory setups with a student-centred focus.   During intensives, the incumbent will be expected to work a variety of shifts within a set roster, ranging from 7:30am – 3:30pm, 8:00am – 4pm and 12:30 – 8:30pm, with the occasional shift ending at 10:00pm.   Please refer to the attached position for a comprehensive list of roles and responsibilities.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through supporting the learning requirements of students to develop invaluable hands-on practical skills in a real-world multidisciplinary environment   About You You are a proactive, highly organised and collaborative professional with experience in clinical simulation and a strong commitment to delivering high-quality clinical education within a university or educational setting. You have a sound understanding of clinical simulation practices and the practical application of equipment, resources and technologies that support effective teaching, learning and assessment. You bring demonstrated experience in preparing, coordinating and supporting clinical teaching and assessment activities across a range of learning levels. As a collaborative team member, you can provide functional supervision, support colleagues and contribute to the efficient coordination of day-to-day operations. Your strong organisational, communication and problem-solving skills enable you to manage complex workflows, meet fixed deadlines and balance competing priorities effectively.   In this role you will also demonstrate:   Qualification in Nursing or Midwifery or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and training relevant to the position. Effective written communication skills, including the capacity to draft correspondence and respond to enquiries autonomously. Excellent oral and interpersonal skills, including the ability to provide effective and appropriate information and advice to a wide range of staff and provide instructions to students. Experience with clinical teaching and assessment set up, including knowledge of and familiarity with equipment used for clinical teaching purposes.    College of Health and Enablement    At the College of Health and Enablement, we aim to be an innovator in contemporary education producing a safer and more resilient health and social care workforce. Our mission is to improve lives and empower communities, enabling better health and caring in the world through an exceptional student learning experience. At the College, our research mission is about co-creating new knowledge which promotes wellbeing, and better care and support, to individuals and communities across the lifespan. We work proactively with industry partners and professional bodies leading to more employable and innovative graduates.      Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet AwardsVibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia).Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice      Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include:   Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. In addition, it is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). SA: Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Senior Manager, Healthcare Simulation Himi Ibrahim or Recruitment Advisor Alessia Rotolo Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 15 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/IDS-Platforms-Analyst---Coordinator_JR0000017879": {
      "title": "IDS Platforms Analyst & Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-28\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Continuing  Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710  Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description   About the Role   The Platforms Analyst & Coordinator works under general to broad direction, and is responsible for contributing to analysis, support and coordination of activities related to the ongoing operation and development of the relevant platforms and applications.    As this is a customer facing role, operating on a diverse range of research specific SAS platforms, you will be responsible for; contributing to requirements analysis activities, interpreting business requirements and translating them into technical requirements - along with supporting business change impact analysis to identify appropriate change management activities to support system changes.   Some of the key position responsibilities are: Supporting the technical change, configuration and release management process for the relevant platforms and applications in alignment with IDS frameworks, principles and standards. Assisting as required with both formal and informal market scanning and benchmarking activities to be aware of emerging market offerings and trends. Contributing to the continuous development of relevant platforms and applications by analysing, configuring, testing and documenting new functionalities and capabilities. Providing timely and effective advice and support to customers and stakeholders by identifying improvements to support services. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to “Changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters”, by 2035 through supporting the stable, reliable and performant operations, assisting our researchers to smoothly access relevant platforms and applications. By working collaboratively across teams and embracing agile ways of working, the role helps deliver efficient, high-quality outcomes and a commitment to excellence and innovation.   About You  The role ideally will suit an early career focused individual with excellent customer service skill. You are committed to continuous improvement and enjoy working in a small, agile team.   While you work independently, you also follow established procedures and have a curious nature.  You will have previous experience in agile ways of working to manage, refine, and prioritise an activity backlog based on relevant customer and organisational priorities, and well-developed, planning, analytical, and critical thinking skills.   See the “How to apply and Information” section at the end of this ad for more about what you will bring to the role.  The ideal candidate will hold a qualification in Information Technology or relevant technical area, and / or equivalent relevant experience in an IT environment.  About Information and Digital Services    Information and Digital Services (IDS) are a pivotal resource of the University, providing information and digital technology services and support to the Flinders University staff and student community. The IDS team apply technical expertise to support university projects and provide core services such as the IDS Service Desk, Audio visual, simulation and event services, Information security and identity access management services and more.    Life at Flinders     They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.    So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice      Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment     Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.    Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy       Prescribed Conditions for Employment     Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University.  The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.   How to Apply and Information    You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 2 pages, and address the below Key Position Capabilities of the  position description: Well-developed thinking, planning and analytical skills Well-developed interpersonal influence and demonstrated ability to negotiate and communicate effectively with staff and customers across a diverse organisation particularly during the design, management and implementation of customer solutions. Experience supporting and maintaining Commercial-Off-The-Shelf (COTS) applications and Software as a Service and enterprise applications and systems. General knowledge and experience in the design and execution of application upgrades including planning, test plan, execution and program close out using good change management principles and  practices. General knowledge of the principles and practices of the software development lifecycle. For more information regarding this position, please contact Scott Anderson Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383 Certification National Police Certificate - Australian Federal Police Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 09 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Programs-and-Events-Officer_JR0000017890": {
      "title": "Programs and Events Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nFixed-term, full-time until 31 Dec 2027. Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role FUSA is seeking a motivated Programs and Events Officer to foster a vibrant community across Flinders Campuses, through the development of programs, events, and activities. Supporting the Senior Coordinator Programming and Communications, you will engage with diverse student groups and individuals to assess needs and co-create solutions that build community and are aimed at improving student experience. You will also be responsible for:  Working closely with other University stakeholders, including student leaders to contribute to the delivery of the social component of a University wide orientation and welcome program. Building relationships with a diverse range of student groups including students from marginalised communities, students with additional needs and student leaders. Engaging in best practice event and project management, including the development and implementation of mechanisms for the ongoing review and effective evaluation of events against key performance indicators. About You You will be an energetic self-starter and excellent communicator who can build positive relationships with a diverse range of students. You will also be a community orientated person, who can create events in partnership with diverse student groups. The successful Programs and Events Officer will also demonstrate: Experience in supporting the development, coordination and delivery of a program of engagement activities, events and activations aligned to placemaking and/or building a vibrant campus culture. Sound knowledge and skills in event and program planning and the delivery processes and techniques employed to achieve required objectives within scope and budget. Tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline with subsequent relevant work experience or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and/or training About Flinders University Student Association (FUSA) Flinders University Student Association (FUSA) is the student organisation that represents the rights and interests of students at Flinders University. FUSA is responsible for community building events on campus, including Orientation.  FUSA also provides a wide range of other services including managing clubs and associations, provides welfare and advocacy services. Life at Flinders    They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.  Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.  Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.  Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. For more information regarding this position, please contact Caleb Osborne Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 10 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Flinders-Rural-Health-SA-Murray-Bridge--Ngarrindjeri-Country/Director--Regional-Training-Hub-SA_JR0000017364-1": {
      "title": "Director, Regional Training Hub SA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Fixed Term until 31 December 2027 | Part-Time at 0.8FTE Academic Level D | $169,706 - $186,706 p.a. (pro-rata) Location: Any RRH SA Campus View Position Description About the Role We are seeking an experienced and influential medical educator and clinician to join Flinders University as an Associate Professor and Director, Regional Training Hub (RTH) SA. This leadership role is responsible for driving excellence in rural medical education, clinical teaching and workforce development across South Australia. Working collaboratively with academic staff, health services, government and industry stakeholders, you will contribute to the clinical years in the MD program, strengthen rural training pathways, support the next generation of medical professionals, and contribute to improved health workforce outcomes in regional and remote communities. Key responsibilities include: Providing strategic leadership for the South Australian Regional Training Hub and the SA Rural Medical Program, ensuring high-quality clinical education, teaching excellence and continuous improvement. Building and maintaining strong partnerships with health services, professional bodies and government stakeholders to develop and strengthen rural medical training pathways and workforce initiatives. Leading and supporting academic staff while delivering innovative, student-centred learning experiences, curriculum development and clinical teaching across the medical program. Mentoring and supporting medical students and junior doctors, promoting rural and remote career pathways through career planning, professional development and placement opportunities. This role is well suited to a local clinician with teaching or academic experience who is looking to further develop their career in higher education, bringing their clinical expertise into a meaningful teaching and academic environment. This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035, through directly contributing to the strategic drivers of Student Centricity and Industry & Enterprise Partnerships, strengthening rural medical education and workforce pathways across South Australia. About You You are a respected clinician and accomplished medical educator with a passion for rural and remote health training. Bringing extensive clinical experience and a strong track record of educational leadership, you thrive in collaborative environments and are committed to developing future medical professionals. You are skilled at building meaningful partnerships, leading high-performing teams, and driving innovation in teaching, curriculum development and workforce initiatives that strengthen health outcomes for regional communities. To be successful in this position, you will demonstrate: Extensive clinical experience and current registration, or eligibility for registration, with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA), supported by a doctorate or equivalent professional standing. A proven record of excellence in tertiary and clinical education, including leadership in the design, delivery and evaluation of innovative teaching and learning practices. Success leading and mentoring academic teams, managing teaching programs, and contributing to curriculum development, quality assurance and accreditation activities. Exceptional communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with students, colleagues, health services, industry partners and community stakeholders. Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA (FRRHSA) facilitates high quality multidisciplinary health education and research in our State's regional areas. We have a strong national and international reputation for providing community-engaged inter-professional training for medical, nursing, and allied health students. This includes the SA Rural Medical (SARM) program and multidisciplinary rural clinical placements. Through this practice, we bring new and exciting opportunities to actively engage and work with rural communities.  Life at Flinders They say know your place. We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy. It is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). A valid South Australian Driver’s License is an essential requirement of this position and must be maintained for the duration of employment. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Claire Drummond . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 15 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Vice-President-and-Executive-Dean_JR0000017864": {
      "title": "Vice-President and Executive Dean, College of Medicine and Public Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Fixed-Term (5 years) Senior Manager Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the opportunity Flinders University is seeking to appoint an outstanding academic leader as its next Vice-President and Executive Dean, College of Medicine and Public Health. This is a significant opportunity to lead one of the University’s largest Colleges, building on a strong record of achievement in education and research while shaping its next phase of growth and development. Reporting to the Vice-Chancellor and serving as a member of the University’s Senior Executive Team, the Vice-President and Executive Dean will provide strategic and academic leadership across the College, advancing research excellence, educational quality and external engagement. A particular priority will be to build on the College’s strong research performance, developing academic capability, strengthening partnerships and driving further growth in research activity and impact. The College has a distinctive national profile, supported by significant investment in health and medical research infrastructure, close partnerships with health services and an extensive medical education footprint across regional and remote Australia, including the Northern Territory. Further opportunities arise through the development of the Flinders HealthCARE Centre and its ambitions for interdisciplinary health education. The successful candidate will play a leading role in realising these opportunities, fostering collaboration across the University and contributing to the delivery of Agenda 2035: Impact. With Purpose. About you You will be a distinguished academic and research leader with a PhD or equivalent qualification, an outstanding record of research achievement and demonstrated success in building and leading high-performing academic environments. You will bring the credibility to engage with the medical and research communities, alongside the strategic, financial and organisational leadership capabilities required to lead a large and complex College. Ambitious, collaborative and collegial, you will have a demonstrated ability to develop others, build productive relationships with clinical, government and research partners, and work effectively across disciplinary boundaries. Importantly, you will bring an institution-wide perspective, with the capacity to contribute to Flinders’ broader ambitions while inspiring the College to achieve even greater success. Life at Flinders Flinders University is entering an exciting period of growth and investment, guided by its Agenda 2035 ambition to change 10 million lives for the better. With a strong commitment to student success, research that matters and meaningful partnerships with industry and communities, Flinders offers an environment in which ambitious leaders can make a lasting contribution. Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide, Flinders offers flexible working arrangements, extensive professional development and a vibrant campus life in Adelaide, one of the world’s most liveable cities. Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. We strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. How to Apply Odgers is assisting Flinders University with this appointment. More information on the role can be found at www.odgers.com/96913 . For further information and a confidential conversation please contact Dr Alistair Clark on Alistair.Clark@odgers.com or Mazin Almaimani on +61 3 8375 7408 or Mazin.Almaimani@odgers.com .  Downloadable Content Candidate Brief Published_Flinders University_VP - Executive Dean, College of Medicine - Public Health.pdf Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 23 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Governance---Policy-Support-Office_JR0000017861": {
      "title": "Governance & Policy Support Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nContinuing | Fixed term (12 months) Higher Education Officer Level 7 | $102,589 - $113,561 p.a. Location: Bedford Park View Position Description About the Role Under broad direction, the Governance & Policy Support Officer supports the effective operation of the University Council, Academic Senate and their subcommittees, by coordinating & hosting meetings, taking minutes, preparing governance documentation and maintaining accurate records and compliance information. The role ensures smooth governance processes through clear communication, efficient logistics and proactive stakeholder support. The role ensures smooth governance processes through clear communication, efficient logistics and proactive stakeholder support, along with maintaining the University’s policy and delegation’s library, assisting policy owners, ensuring governance documents remain current and consistent, and contributes to other important governance and policy projects. The role will also handle Freedom of Information enquiries (following training being provided). Key responsibilities include: Supporting the General Counsel & University Secretary to ensure the effective and efficient operation of Council, Academic Senate and their sub-committees Supporting the Senior Policy and Projects Officer to ensure the effective operation of the University’s policy framework Maintaining accurate records, registers and compliance information relating to the University’s external entities About You This is a terrific opportunity for someone who is interested in learning and enjoys interacting with the decision makers (executive and board) within an organisation. The role ensures smooth governance processes through clear communication, efficient logistics and proactive stakeholder support, so you will bring high level interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, along with the ability to negotiate and liaise with a broad range of stakeholders. In addition, you should also demonstrate: Experience in, and understanding of, governance practices and principles. Proven ability to prepare complex correspondence, papers, briefings, minutes and reports with a high degree of accuracy and attention to detail Competency in using a range of computer applications, including word-processing, Excel, Teams and Zoom, and accessing information from websites, spreadsheets and database It is expected that the successful candidate will have completed a degree and have subsequent relevant experience; or possess an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and/or training. Even if you don’t have all the listed experience, we encourage you to apply if you feel this role aligns with your strengths About Governance, Legal and Risk Governance, Legal and Risk provide governance, legal, policy, compliance, insurance and risk management support to the University and its staff. The Governance, Legal and Risk team provides advice on university governance and decision-making processes and plays a pivotal role in ensuring that Flinders has policies and processes in place to protect its people, assets, and activities. Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and change makers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice      Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.    Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan    Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment A current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check which is satisfactory to the University will be required by Flinders University before the successful applicant can commence in this position . A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.     How to Apply and Information You are required to submit cover letter detailing your suitability as per the position description. For more information regarding this position, please contact Marc Davies Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 03 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Integration-Specialist_JR0000017855": {
      "title": "Senior Integration Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Continuing  Higher Education Officer Level 8 | $116,349 - $128,806 Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description   About the Role   The Senior Integration Specialist is the hands-on technical lead for integration services within IDS. Working under broad direction as part of the IDS integration capability hub, you will design, build, test, implement and support integration solutions in accordance with IDS integration architecture principles, guardrails and design standards. This role is focused on technical delivery and operational reliability. Integration architecture strategy and roadmap ownership sit with our Integration and Solution Architects, and you will work closely with them to turn that direction into secure, dependable solutions that you are accountable for delivering and supporting. Your key responsibilities will include:   Integration delivery and lifecycle: Design, build, test, deploy and maintain secure, scalable integrations on Boomi or equivalent, managing change, release, version control and upgrades across the full lifecycle. Operational reliability: Own production integration services, monitoring performance, resolving incidents and root causes, and ensuring integrations meet agreed service levels. Security, standards and assurance: Build security, privacy and audit requirements into every solution, and conduct design reviews that validate compliance with IDS integration principles, guardrails and standards. Capability and continuous improvement: Maintain reusable patterns, API standards and technical documentation, and use service metrics to improve the reliability, supportability and cost-effectiveness of integration services. Collaboration and technical leadership: Work with Integration and Solution Architects, product owners, project teams and vendors, providing design guidance and mentoring to promote consistent engineering practice.   By delivering dependable integrations that connect systems and data across Flinders, the role underpins digital transformation and helps ensure technology is implemented in an ethical, sustainable and agile way.   About You   You are a hands-on integration professional who enjoys solving complex technical problems and takes pride in building solutions that are secure, well documented and easy to support. In this role you will own technical outcomes, ensure production reliability, exercise sound technical judgement and provide technical leadership across the integration services you deliver.   Your analytical skills are matched by strong written communication, including the ability to produce quality design documents, interface specifications, work instructions and change requests. You build strong working relationships and communicate clearly with technical and non-technical audiences, using your influence and negotiation skills to guide stakeholders, project teams and vendors through the design, delivery and support of integration solutions.   You also possess:   Extensive hands-on experience delivering application and data integration solutions across the full lifecycle, from requirements and design through testing, deployment and transition to support. Proven expertise developing, configuring and supporting an enterprise integration platform (such as Boomi or equivalent), with strong knowledge of API design, integration patterns, data mapping and error handling. Demonstrated ownership of production integration services, ensuring they are reliable, scalable and maintainable through incident resolution, root cause analysis, performance optimisation and operational reporting. A sound understanding of integration security, privacy, access control and audit requirements, and the ability to undertake technical design reviews that assess compliance with architecture principles, guardrails and standards, and recommend corrective actions. Experience with integration testing, release management and change control, and with improving capability through reusable patterns, automation, documentation and continuous improvement. Demonstrated technical leadership through design guidance, knowledge sharing and mentoring, and experience delivering in agile, scaled agile or matrix environments.   The ideal candidate will also hold an appropriate tertiary qualification in Information Technology or a relevant technical area, and / or equivalent relevant experience in an IT environment. Experience in the higher education sector will be highly regarded.   About Information and Digital Services     Information and Digital Services (IDS) are a pivotal resource of the University, providing information and digital technology services and support to the Flinders University staff and student community. The IDS team apply technical expertise to support university projects and provide core services such as the IDS Service Desk, Audio visual, simulation and event services, Information security and identity access management services and more.     Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice       Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment      Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.     Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy        Prescribed Conditions for Employment      Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.     This includes a current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and a Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks.  A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.     How to Apply and Information     You are required to submit a CV, along with a Cover Letter as a separate document, telling us how your experiences have set you up for success, and why you are the right person for the job. For more information regarding this position, please contact  James Reed Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383.  Certification National Police Check - Australian Federal Police Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 05 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Sustainability-Coordinator_JR0000017834": {
      "title": "Sustainability Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Continuing Professional HEO6 | $94,456 - $100,710 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   Flinders University is bringing its 2030 Sustainability Strategy to life through practical action across climate, biodiversity, resource efficiency, sustainable campus operations and community engagement. We are seeking a collaborative and proactive Sustainability Coordinator to help deliver a diverse range of projects and initiatives that embed sustainability across the University.   Based at Bedford Park, but working across our campuses, you'll connect with staff, students, academics and external partners to translate sustainability ambitions into meaningful outcomes. From engaging our community and supporting environmental initiatives to coordinating projects and improving everyday practices, this is a varied role where your contribution will make a tangible difference. We are the first institution in the world to achieve a coveted Gold rating under the newly released version 3 of the international Sustainability Tracking, Assessment & Rating System. To learn more about this award and our sustainability initiatives, head over to our website: Sustainability at Flinders About the role Reporting to the Head of Sustainability, you'll coordinate a diverse range of initiatives supporting Flinders University's 2030 Sustainability Strategy, working collaboratively across academic and professional areas. You'll play an important role in connecting people, coordinating practical projects, communicating sustainability achievements and supporting the effective operation of the Sustainability team. Your work will contribute to a wide range of projects and outcomes, including: Deliver sustainability projects: Coordinate initiatives across climate action, biodiversity, resource recovery, sustainable food systems, water efficiency, active transport and sustainable procurement. Engage and inspire our community: Deliver staff and student engagement programs, orientation activities, Sustainability Week, workshops, volunteering opportunities and behaviour change campaigns. Communicate sustainability achievements: Develop newsletters, digital content, educational resources and communications that promote sustainability initiatives, research and positive outcomes. Support climate resilience and biodiversity: Coordinate working groups, monitoring activities, stakeholder workshops and practical initiatives that enhance campus sustainability and climate adaptation. Connect people and partnerships: Work with academic staff, students and external organisations to support sustainability-related teaching, research, experiential learning and community projects. Support sustainability governance and operations: Coordinate meetings, project documentation, reporting and administrative activities, including procurement, budgets and records management For a more comprehensive list of Key Position Responsibilities, please view the Position Description About You You're a proactive, organised and collaborative sustainability professional who enjoys translating ideas into practical action and bringing people together to achieve positive outcomes. You have strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, an adaptable approach and the ability to coordinate multiple activities across a diverse organisation. You're equally comfortable delivering a project, facilitating a workshop, developing communications or supporting the day-to-day operations of a busy team. You will bring: Experience coordinating sustainability, environmental or organisational improvement projects, including managing activities, budgets, timelines and deliverables. A sound understanding of contemporary sustainability principles and practices, including climate action, biodiversity, resource efficiency and sustainable operations. Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills, with the ability to engage effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Experience coordinating communications, engagement activities, workshops, events or behaviour change initiatives. Well-developed written communication skills, including preparing reports, project documentation, digital communications and educational resources. Strong organisational and administrative skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and support procurement, financial and operational processes. Experience implementing sustainability initiatives within a university, public sector or similarly complex organisation will be highly regarded. The ideal candidate will hold a degree in sustainability, environmental management, environmental science, communications or a related discipline, together with relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training   Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.     Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.     Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.     Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.    So,  why work at Flinders?    17% Superannuation + salary packaging options    Flexible working arrangements    Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program   Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers     We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.    Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).    Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice       Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.      How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description For more information regarding this position, please contact William Van Ausdal Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 04 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Research-Assistant_JR0000017841": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Fixed-Term (12 months) | Part Time (0.5FTE) Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 (pro rata) Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   About the Role Join the Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute and contribute to the CRESCENT Study, an exciting research project investigating how light sensitivity and light exposure patterns may influence the development of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) following traumatic injury. This is a unique opportunity to work closely with the Trauma Service Team at Flinders Medical Centre and provide hands-on support to the Postdoctoral Research Associate and Chief Investigator in the delivery of this important research. Working directly with participants throughout their research journey, you will play a key role in recruitment, assessments and follow-up activities that contribute to a better understanding of trauma, PTSD and recovery.   This position is offered at 0.5 FTE, with flexibility required across multiple weekdays to support participant scheduling and the operational needs of the study.   As a Research Assistant, you will be responsible for: Supporting participant identification, recruitment and enrolment for the CRESCENT Study. Conducting research interviews, assessments and participant follow-up activities. Coordinating research activities between participants, hospital staff and the research team. Maintaining accurate study records, research data and documentation in line with approved protocols.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through advancing Research that Matters. The role supports research that aims to improve our understanding of trauma, PTSD and recovery, ultimately contributing to better health outcomes for individuals and communities.   About You   You are a compassionate and organised research professional who enjoys working directly with people and making a meaningful contribution to health research. Comfortable building rapport in sensitive situations, you bring empathy, professionalism and a trauma-informed approach to participant interactions. You are confident working both independently and collaboratively and are motivated by the opportunity to support research that has a genuine impact on people's lives and wellbeing.   To thrive in this role, you will bring: A degree in psychology, health sciences, medicine, social sciences, public health or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of experience and training. Demonstrated experience working with people in professional settings, particularly where conversations may involve sensitive or potentially distressing experiences. The ability to build strong relationships through effective communication, interpersonal skills and a collaborative approach. Excellent organisational skills, attention to detail, and the ability to manage competing priorities while working independently. Experience within hospital, trauma, mental health or related clinical settings, and/or a demonstrated interest in trauma-informed research and practice will be highly regarded.   College of Medicine and Public Health      At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place. We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide.   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Dayna Easton Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 05 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Content-Producer--Alumni---Advancement_JR0000017840": {
      "title": "Content Producer, Alumni & Advancement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Fixed Term (2 years) Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role When we tell you this job will make a difference, we mean it! This newly created fixed-term position plays a key role in supporting the development and delivery of engaging content that strengthens relationships with donors, alumni and supporters while showcasing the impact of philanthropy at Flinders University. Working across both Alumni and Advancement portfolios, you'll contribute to communications that support the University's ambitious vision and help bring major initiatives, including the Flinders HealthCARE Centre project, to life. Reporting to the Marketing & Communications Partner, Advancement, you'll create compelling stories and multimedia content that inspire support, build engagement and celebrate the achievements of our alumni and donor communities. You'll have the opportunity to produce content across a diverse range of channels, including web, social media, email campaigns, publications, photography and video, while helping ensure our digital presence remains current, accessible and aligned with best-practice standards Your Key Position Responsibilities include: Developing and delivering engaging content across digital and print channels, including websites, social media, EDMs, newsletters, publications and campaign materials. Writing, editing and proof-reading content tailored to a range of audiences, including alumni, donors, community partners and stakeholders. Producing dynamic multimedia content, including photography, video and digital assets that support Alumni and Advancement initiatives. Maintaining and updating website content in line with brand, accessibility, UX/UI and SEO best practice.   For a comprehensive list of Key Position Responsibilities, please view the Position Description     About You You are a creative and versatile content specialist who enjoys transforming stories into engaging communications that connect with audiences and achieve meaningful outcomes. With experience creating content across multiple channels, you combine exceptional writing and editing skills with a strong understanding of digital communications, audience engagement and multimedia production. You are highly organised, collaborative and able to manage competing priorities while maintaining exceptional attention to detail. To be successful in this role, you will demonstrate: A talent for emotive storytelling that grabs readers’ attention and answers the question “why does this matter?” Strong experience writing, editing and proof-reading content for online and print audiences. Experience creating multimedia content, including photography and video. Proficiency in creative production tools such as Adobe Photoshop, Acrobat and Canva. Experience using content management systems (preferably AEM), email marketing platforms (preferably Campaign Monitor) and SEO principles. Strong stakeholder engagement skills and the ability to work collaboratively across diverse teams.   Experience working within a higher education, membership, community or not-for-profit environment will be highly regarded. About the Office of Alumni and Advancement   The Alumni and Advancement team exists to foster enduring relationships with alumni, community members, donors, and stakeholders, thereby enhancing Flinders University’s impact locally and globally. Through a whole-of-university approach, we champion a vibrant culture of philanthropy and engagement, securing vital financial support for students and pioneering research and adding value to our graduates’ lives at every stage of life. In alignment with the University’s overarching vision, the Alumni and Advancement team is dedicated to building a connected, supportive, and influential Flinders community.   Life at Flinders   We're transforming and investing in people and facilities to create contemporary, stimulating, and satisfying learning and work environments that reflect our core values of excellence, innovation, courage, and integrity. Flinders is refocusing its strategic priorities with the aim of elevating its performance to be a top ten Australian university, and amongst the top 1% in the world.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proudly committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy and we strive to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Flinders University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive environment, free from gender‑based violence. Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre‑employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   This includes a Gender‑Based Violence declaration, in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender‑Based Violence (2025), and a Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check. A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.   undefined   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, a cover letter including a proactive statement about why you are suitable for the role and a Suitability Statement as a separate document, which addresses the below Key Position Capabilities of the position description : Demonstrated experience in donor relations, stewardship or a related field within a higher education or non-profit environment. Exemplary written communication skills including experience in writing, editing and proof-reading content for online and print channels tailored to diverse audiences. Proven experience in Adobe Photoshop, Acrobat and InDesign for creative production & experience in photography and video production Experience in Content Management Systems (preferably AEM), email management (preferably Campaign Monitor) and SEO to improve reach. General knowledge of industry best practice principles and standards for digital content across web, social and email marketing. Including the ability to interpret and apply relevant policies and procedures, including content delivery standards, usability and accessibility. For more information regarding this position, please contact Heidi Warren Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged, and are able to, as far as practicably possible, participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, including being accompanied by an accredited assistance animal with public access rights as per the Disability Policy , please advise the contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 04 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Lecturer-in-Women-s-and-Gender-Studies_JR0000017832": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Women's and Gender Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full Time | Continuing appointment Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 pa | 17% Super View Position Description Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country About the Role: We are seeking an innovative and collaborative Lecturer in Women’s and Gender Studies to contribute to high-quality teaching, research, and engagement across the College of Human Sciences and Culture. This is an exciting opportunity for an academic who is committed to interdisciplinary scholarship, contemporary teaching practices, and preparing students to make a positive difference in society. In this role, you will deliver engaging undergraduate and/or postgraduate teaching across the Women’s and Gender Studies suite, developing innovative learning materials and applying contemporary, student-centred teaching approaches across face-to-face and online environments. You will contribute to a vibrant research culture through independent, high-quality research and/or creative activity, including scholarly publications, external research funding, and research student supervision. For a full list of the key position responsibilities, please refer to the position description . This position is open to those with Australian working rights. Relocation support will be available. About You To be successful in this role, you will have completed a Phd, EdD or an equivalent qualification. The successful candidate will also require: Demonstrated evidence of, or capacity to, undertake high-quality teaching in a tertiary environment, including involvement in topic coordination and curriculum design. Evidence of successful and independent innovation in learning and teaching delivery or design, including use of e-learning technologies. Evidence of a student-centred approach. Demonstrated experience or capability to engage with students (and deliver teaching modules) via online learning platforms.   For a full list of the key position capabilities of the successful candidate, please refer to the position description .   College of Human Sciences and Culture   Through our world-class teaching and research, we equip students with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and advance human development. The College is dedicated to cultivating bright minds and inspiring innovative, passionate, and highly skilled graduates for future ready careers, and delivering connections for life.  Life at Flinders Flinders University is driven by a bold purpose: to change lives and change the world. Through Agenda 2035 – Impact. With Purpose. , we are expanding the scale, reach, and ambition of our research to deliver meaningful outcomes for communities, industry, and global health systems. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Wide range of professional development activities and services We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia). Support future initiatives like the Flinders HealthCARE Centre Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.    In addition, it is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working With Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA).    How to Apply and Information As well as a CV, you are required to submit a Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . For more information regarding this position, please contact Prof Kate Douglas . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 04 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Associate-Lecturer---Lecturer-in-Indigenous-Knowledges-and-Culture_JR0000017827": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Indigenous Knowledges and Culture",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time Position (36.75 hours per week) | Fixed Term until end of December 2027 Academic Level A | $90,930 - $110,197 p.a. + 17% superannuation Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 p.a. + 17% superannuation Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Level A Position Description View Level B Position Description   Acknowledgement of Country:    Flinders University acknowledges the Traditional Owners and Custodians of the lands on which its campuses are located. These are the Traditional Lands of the Arrernte, Dagoman, First Nations of the South East, First Peoples of the River Murray & Mallee region, Jawoyn, Kaurna, Larrakia, Ngadjuri, Ngarrindjeri, Ramindjeri, Warumungu, Wardaman and Yolngu people.    We honour their Elders past, present and emerging.    The successful candidate will be appointed to either the Level A or Level B position. Please indicate on your application which position(s) you would like to be considered for. About the Positions: We are seeking a vibrant and collaborative Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Indigenous Knowledges and Culture to join our growing Aboriginal Health team. In this exciting role, you will contribute to excellence in teaching and learning across the suite of cultural education topics, as well as contributing to teaching and the scholarship of teaching using innovations in teaching practices and technologies to deliver excellence in teaching. The successful candidate will possess cultural authority and will deliver education relating to Indigenous knowledges and cultural practices and will facilitate academic and logistic support to Indigenous students in the College and will work with members of the Indigenous Health Education Team to play a role in Flinders engagement with community, including consultation.   You will also have the exciting opportunity to: Create, coordinate and conduct inspiring teaching informed by approaches that enhance the student experience, with a focus on cultural education topics, promoting the principles of primary health care, cultural safety and an interprofessional approach to Indigenous Health education, across all levels and modes of the College. Contribute to the planning and evaluation of these topics, working with Topic and Course Coordinators as required. Make an independent contribution to the review, renewal, development and design of curricula and courses that are attractive to existing and new cohorts of students and that nurture creativity and critical thinking skills.   About You (Level A)   You are an emerging teaching professional who has experience in teaching and learning outputs in the areas of Indigenous health issues, cultural safety, primary health care and population health, and is ready to use your skillset in a tertiary educational environment. Your passion for cultural education, paired with your deep understanding of the nexus between teaching and learning, research and scholarship effortlessly enables you to create a nurturing and student-centred learning experience. Your continued commitment to community recognition of Indigenous knowledges and practices further highlights your sheer passion for sharing Cultural knowledge. You are also actively connected to community and/or having experience working within Aboriginal services and organisations that allow you to bring a deeper, sharper understanding of cultural safety, community perspectives and the realities of working in these spaces, effortless strengthening the quality and relevancy of your teaching and connection to students.   To succeed in this role, you will also be able to demonstrate the following: Satisfactory completion of 4 years tertiary study or equivalent qualifications and experience in the area of teaching For Indigenous staff members whose duties involve cultural activity which prioritises Indigenous knowledges and practices requiring community cultural recognition: evidence of such recognition plus completion or near completion of a higher degree study or substantive experience in some branch of Indigenous studies/education. Lived experience and/or experience working within Aboriginal communities, as well as established relationships with relevant groups and organisations, including the ability to lead community consultation. Demonstrates a commitment to fostering a positive workplace culture, and role modelling behaviours that support the University’s Reconciliation Action Plan (RAP) and commitment to enhancing educational opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Evidence of successful learning and teaching delivery or design, including use of e-learning technologies, as well as the engage with students (and deliver teaching modules) via online learning platforms.   About You (Level B)   You are an established teaching professional who has significant experience in teaching and learning outcomes at a tertiary level and is renowned for your excellence in teaching that is informed by approaches that genuinely enhance the student experience. Your experience in the independent innovation in learning and teaching delivery and/or design, inclusive of e-learning technologies, allows you to meet students where they are on their learning journey, regardless of their personal or professional demands. You bring knowledge from evidenced professional development of teaching, including peer or supervisor reviews of teaching and/or external accreditation processes, strengthening the quality of your teaching outcomes.   You are also actively connected to community and/or having experience working within Aboriginal services and organisations that allow you to bring a deeper, sharper understanding of cultural safety, community perspectives and the realities of working in these spaces, effortless strengthening the quality and relevancy of your teaching and connection to students. In addition to this, your experience will also highlight the following: Completion of a PhD or EdD, or equivalent qualification For Indigenous staff members whose duties involve cultural activity which prioritises Indigenous knowledges and practices requiring community cultural recognition: evidence of such recognition plus completion or near completion of a higher degree study or substantive experience in some branch of Indigenous studies/education. Lived experience and/or experience working within Aboriginal communities, as well as established relationships with relevant groups and organisations, including the ability to lead community consultation. Demonstrates a commitment to fostering a positive workplace culture, and role modelling behaviours that support the University’s Reconciliation Action Plan (RAP) and commitment to enhancing educational opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Demonstrated contribution to academic quality assurance processes, such as topic or course review, and experience in coordinating and/or leading the teaching activities of other staff. Experience with engagement within a relevant external professional domain and within a wider community context at local, national or international level which could include working parties, committees, professional associations and conferenced workshops.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters , by 2035 through the Value of Excellence. This value is demonstrated through the delivery of high-quality, innovative, and culturally responsive teaching that the Associate Lecturer / Lecturer works towards. The role advances student learning outcomes, contributes to the scholarship of teaching, supports Indigenous student success, and strengthens community engagement, reflecting a commitment to excellence in education, cultural leadership, and continuous improvement.   College of Medicine and Public Health             At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Genuine flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities for either the Level A Position Description or Level B Position Description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Larissa Taylor . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 03 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
