    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park/Casual-Academic-Register---College-of-Humanities--Arts-and-Social-Sciences_JR0000000090": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Register – College of Humanities, Arts and Social Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2024-11-18\nPart time\n\nAbout Flinders Our bold vision, captured in our Strategic Plan: Making a Difference: The 2025 Agenda, is to be internationally recognised as a world leader in research, an innovator in contemporary education, and the source of Australia’s most enterprising graduates. To realise this ambition, we recently made a significant organisational change to a six College structure with a professional staff and services alignment. We recognise the key to our success is exceptional people and we’re seeking an outstanding individual to join the leadership team of our transformed university. Casual Register Flinders University has a number of Casual Registers as a means of expressions of interest for the purpose of casual work, should such opportunities arise. How to Register: Upload your current CV/Resumé detailing your qualifications and experience. You may choose to upload a Cover Letter of no more than 1 page to support your CV/Resumé. Your application will be acknowledged by an automated e-mail once submitted. If a casual opportunity is available and your application is considered suitable, the relevant hiring manager will contact you for a further discussion. Please note, only suitable applicants will be contacted. Applying to the Casual Register does not guarantee employment. Further Information: Pursuant to Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA) this position may be deemed prescribed.  It is an inherent requirement of a prescribed position that the successful candidate maintains a current Working With Children Check which is satisfactory to the University. Please contact Recruitment@Flinders  by email at recruitment@flinders.edu.au  should you have any queries regarding Casual opportunities."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Lecturer-in-Finance_JR0000017980": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-05\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Continuing position Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 pa Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role We are seeking a dedicated Lecturer (Teaching Specialist) with a student-centred ethos to develop creative, enterprising and career-ready graduates. The Lecturer in Finance will contribute to excellence in teaching and learning across the suite of Business and Finance topics. The position will be predominantly concerned with teaching and teaching-related duties involving the creation, delivery and continuous quality improvement of the topics and programs offered by the University. The key responsibilities of the position will include, but is not limited to: Create, coordinate and conduct inspiring teaching informed by approaches that enhance the student experience Contribute to the planning and evaluation of these topics, undertaking the role of Topic Coordinator as required. Make an independent contribution to the review, renewal, development and design of curricula and courses that are attractive to existing and new cohorts of students and that nurture creativity and critical-thinking skills O ffshore teaching may also be required, as per the College needs. This position will be open to those with existing Australian working rights. Relocation support is available for the successful candidate.   About You The successful candidate will demonstrate significant teaching experience and excellence at a tertiary level, and should have successfully completed a PhD or EdD in Business, Finance or a related discipline. The successful candidate will also demonstrate the following key position capabilities, among others: Evidence of use of learning and teaching approaches which enhance student success. Evidence of teaching and assessing practical legal skills. Evidence of student-centred approach. Evidence of successful and independent innovation in learning and teaching delivery or design, including use of e-learning technologies.   About the College/Portfolio Through our world-class teaching and research, we equip students with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and advance human development. The College is dedicated to cultivating bright minds and inspiring innovative, passionate, and highly skilled graduates for future ready careers, and delivering connections for life.    Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.     How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . For more information regarding this position, please contact Philip Palmer . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 02 Dec 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Lecturer-in-Health-Sciences_JR0000017977-1": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Health Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-05\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Continuing Academic Teaching & Research Level C | $141,370 - $162,619 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role A key member of academic staff in the area of Health Sciences, the Senior Lecturer will undertake a leading role in the planning and delivery of topics, including development of teaching materials, delivery of lectures/ tutorials, curriculum development or other teaching strategies, with a focus on Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health. Additionally, the Senior Lecturer will make a significant contribution to ethical, high quality and innovative research through activities such as scholarship, quality publication, external grant acquisition, mentoring and developing early career researchers and presentations that align with the Caring Futures Institute. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through collaboration, participation in cross-disciplinary research activity as well as exploring new partnerships with relevant internal and external stakeholders College of Health and Enablement    At the College of Health and Enablement, we aim to be an innovator in contemporary education producing a safer and more resilient health and social care workforce. Our mission is to improve lives and empower communities, enabling better health and caring in the world through an exceptional student learning experience. At the College, our research mission is about co-creating new knowledge which promotes wellbeing, and better care and support, to individuals and communities across the lifespan. We work proactively with industry partners and professional bodies leading to more employable and innovative graduates.      About You You’re an exceptional communicator, both written and verbal, with the ability to build and maintain effective working relationships across diverse teams. Your leadership and supervisory experience reflect your strength in guiding high-performing, multi-disciplinary teams and fostering a collaborative, respectful workplace. With a strong track record in research (for career stage) in an area of strategic research focus for the Caring Futures Institute, you bring evidence of sustained, high-quality publications—often as senior author—and a proven ability to secure competitive external grants. In this role you will also be able to demonstrate: Hold and maintain a current membership with a relevant professional association. Experience in the area of teaching or equivalent professional and cultural expertise relevant to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health. Excellent oral and written communication skills and interpersonal skills, including the demonstrated ability to establish effective relationships with staff, students and industry nationally and/or internationally. Evidence of knowledge and practice in the field of First Nations health, with the ability to facilitate student learning as it relates to cultural safety and responsiveness. Completion of a PhD, or equivalent qualification in an area relevant to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Health or any one of the following attached to the position description.     Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide. Ninety per cent of Flinders research has been ranked at ‘world standard or above’ by Excellence in Research for Australia (ERA). Wide range of professional development activities, funds and services, such as Flinders University’s Researcher Development and Leadership Programs. Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include:   Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. SA: Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Professor Chris Barr Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 19 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Tonsley/Digital-Content-Officer_JR0000017976": {
      "title": "Digital Content Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-05\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Part-time (0.8) | Fixed-term until July 2027 Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 pa Location: Tonsley Campus / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role The Digital Content Officer will provide digital content and resource development support for the Disability Employment Research Team. The successful candidate will work alongside the project lead to develop, update and maintain accessible digital information and resources to assist industry partners in understanding and participating in the Talent Access Partnerships project. This project is expected to engage more than 300 employers, and the Digital Content Officer will be required to prepare and adapt digital information and resources to support multiple project partners and provide consistent communication throughout the project period. The responsibilities of this position will include, but is not limited to: Develop, edit and format digital information resources for employers and project stakeholders using established communication and accessibility guidelines. Utilise and adapt AI to develop assistive technologies, including websites and applications. Assist with developing digital information packages to support employer participation in the TAP project. Adapt existing project information into clear, accessible and audience-appropriate digital content with approved project messaging. This position will be open to those with existing Australian working rights. Relocation support is available for the successful candidate. About You The successful candidate will demonstrate a relevant degree and/or experience in preparing, editing or publishing digital content as well as a demonstrated ability to write and edit clear, engaging digital content. The successful candidate will also demonstrate the following key position capabilities, among others: Ability to adapt information for diverse audiences and digital channels. Knowledge of core digital content principles such as accessibility, plain language, and usability. Skilled in organising tasks, managing competing priorities, and meeting deadlines. Experience working with website content management systems, social media platforms or other digital communication tools.   Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . For this position, we also require a portfolio or examples of your previous work. For more information regarding this position, please contact Jung Yoon . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 18 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Business-Improvement-Analyst_JR0000017970-1": {
      "title": "Senior Business Improvement Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nFull time\n\nContinuing | Full time Higher Education Officer Level 8 | $116,349 - $128,806 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role This newly created role responds to ongoing system, regulatory and service change, and will support sustainability and continuous improvement across Student Administration Services. A unique opportunity at Flinders, the role suits someone with business analysis experience in the information technology space who will work closely with Information and Digital Services (IDS) and the business during a period of significant innovation, including the transition of our timetabling system from an on-premises solution to the cloud. You will provide high-level business analysis, process improvement and system assurance capability for Student Administration Services and the broader Student Experience and Management portfolio. Based within Student Administration Services, you will work with business owners, Student Experience and Management units and IDS to identify operational, process and system gaps, translate business needs into clear requirements, and support the design and adoption of practical, governed solutions. You will help reduce reliance on unsupported local tools by improving process design, system assurance, documentation and decision-making across core administrative functions Some of the responsibilities of the position are: Translating operational requirements into practical process and system improvements, including AI opportunities; Supporting major University projects affecting student administration; Reducing manual handling, duplication, and key-person dependency; Strengthening system testing, implementation readiness, and governance; and Improving service consistency, staff capacity, and student outcomes. For a more comprehensive list, please view the Position Description About You You have experience in Higher Education and understand how systems and data work / interrelate across various platforms, governance & risk and working with significant data sets.  Your exemplary analytical, problem-solving and strategic thinking skills include the ability to investigate complex issues, evaluate options and develop practical recommendations that support organisational objectives. Your high-level interpersonal, communication and stakeholder management skills, include the ability to influence, negotiate and build productive relationships across diverse stakeholder groups, ensuring you are able to translate complex business, technical and process information into practical outcomes. This position requires you to have demonstrated ability to assess and apply artificial intelligence tools in a responsible and practical way to improve business analysis, process documentation, workflow design, data analysis and service improvement, while maintaining appropriate governance, privacy, security and human oversight. About the Office of the Deputy Vice Chancellor (Students) The Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Students) provides administrative support to key strategic and operational functions of the University’s student-related and academic services. Under the general portfolio, the Office team support Portfolio leadership and facilitate dialogue between key service areas to help the Portfolio ensure Flinders’ educational offerings are innovative, of assured quality, and are student centred. This drives our values and ethos, our impact and engagement, and our vision to be internationally recognised as a world leader in research, an innovator in contemporary education, and the source of Australia’s most enterprising graduates. Life at Flinders    They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.  Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.  Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.  Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University.The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role . How to Apply and Information You are required to provide your Resumé, along with a separate cover letter, telli ng us why this role interests you and how your experience within higher education makes you the ideal candidate You are highly encouraged to contact Luke Robins for more information regarding this position Certification National Police Certificate - Australian Federal Police Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 16 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Professor-of-Genomics_JR0000016620": {
      "title": "Professor of Genomics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-04\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full Time | Fixed Term for 5 years Academic Level E | $217,876 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description College of Medicine and Public Health At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do. About the Role The Professor of Genomics is a senior academic leadership position responsible for driving internationally competitive research and shaping the strategic direction of genomics within the College of Medicine and Public Health. Working collaboratively with academic, industry and government partners, the position plays a key role in securing major research funding, building multidisciplinary capability, and developing the next generation of researchers.   Key responsibilities include: Leading and delivering internationally recognised, high-impact genomics research programs and securing significant external funding. Setting strategic research priorities and expanding the University’s genomics capability and reputation. Building and sustaining strong collaborations with industry, government, health services and academic partners. Mentoring and developing higher degree research students, fellows and academic staff to foster a high-performing research culture. In addition to the above, Flinders is now proudly home to the tremendously successful South Australian Genomics Centre (SAGC) , which is expanding byway of an acquisition of the highly automated genomics laboratory – propositioning the highest-throughput and lowest-cost genomics capabilities in the Southern Hemisphere. This is a once in a lifetime opportunity for a senior Genomics academic to lead SAGC as new Scientific Director and the new facility. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters , by 2035 through directly contributing to the Strategic Driver of Research that Matters and fostering Innovation and Excellence, by leading impactful genomics research that delivers meaningful health, biomedical and population outcomes while strengthening global collaboration and translation. About You You are an internationally recognised genomics researcher and academic leader with a distinguished track record of delivering high-impact research and securing substantial competitive funding. You bring deep expertise in leading large-scale research programs, fostering multidisciplinary collaboration, and translating research outcomes into real-world impact. Passionate about developing future researchers, you are committed to mentoring emerging academics and building a strong, sustainable research culture aligned with institutional priorities. Your attributes and accomplishments include: A PhD in genomics or a closely related discipline, with an outstanding international research reputation and sustained record of high-quality publications and competitive grant success. The ability to think strategically and identify emerging opportunities that translate into impactful research initiatives. A collaborative mindset, with a natural ability to build trusted relationships and align diverse stakeholders around shared goals. A demonstrated commitment to supervising and mentoring higher degree students and early- to mid-career researchers, fostering a high-performing research environment.   Life at Flinders They say know your place. We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options. Genuine flexible working arrangements. Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program. Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers.  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice. Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide.   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Professor Jill Carr. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 03 Dec 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Associate-Lecturer---Professor-in-Media---Communications_JR0000017953": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer - Professor in Media & Communications",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-11-03\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Multiple Full-Time, Continuing Academic Opportunities Available Academic Level A | $90,930 - $110,197 Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 Academic Level C | $141,370 - $162,619 Academic Level D | $169,706 - $186,706 Academic Level E | $217,876 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country Teaching & Research Position Descriptions: Level A | Level B | Level C | Level D | Level E Teaching Specialist Position Descriptions: Level A | Level B | Level C | Level D | Level E   Multiple opportunities are available across both Teaching & Research and Teaching Specialist Academic pathways at Academic Levels A-E. Please indicate in your application which level(s) and pathway(s) you would like to be considered for. About the Roles Join a dynamic and collaborative Media and Communications team and help shape the future of the discipline through exceptional teaching, impactful research, educational innovation and industry engagement. We are seeking talented academics across a range of career stages and pathways, from Associate Lecturer to Professor, including both Teaching & Research and Teaching Specialist appointments. These opportunities provide the chance to inspire students, contribute to contemporary and industry-connected curricula, advance disciplinary knowledge and practice, and strengthen the University's reputation for excellence in teaching, learning and research. Candidates with expertise in Journalism, Digital Media and/or Corporate Communication are encouraged to apply. Deliver engaging, contemporary and student-centred teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, contributing to curriculum innovation and an outstanding student experience. Contribute to the advancement of knowledge and practice through research, scholarship, educational innovation, professional engagement and curriculum development, aligned with your academic pathway and level. Build and maintain strong partnerships with industry, professional bodies and community stakeholders to enhance student learning, graduate employability and disciplinary impact. Contribute to the leadership, growth and success of the discipline through academic citizenship, mentoring, quality assurance and strategic initiatives, with responsibilities commensurate with academic level and experience. These positions make a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan, changing 10 million Lives: Every One Matters, by 2035, through directly contributing to our strategic drivers of Student Centricity, Research that Matters, and Industry & Enterprise Partnerships.   About You You are a passionate educator, researcher, industry practitioner or academic leader who is committed to excellence in Media and Communications. You bring expertise in your discipline, a collaborative mindset and a dedication to delivering outstanding outcomes for students. Whether your strengths lie in research and scholarship, educational leadership, curriculum innovation or industry engagement, you will be motivated by the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to a contemporary and student-centred academic environment. Relevant qualifications in Media, Communications, Journalism, Digital Media, Corporate Communication or a related discipline. Demonstrated ability to deliver engaging, high-quality teaching and learning experiences, including the application of contemporary pedagogical practices and learning technologies. Evidence of achievement and impact through research, scholarship, educational leadership, curriculum development, professional practice and/or industry engagement, appropriate to your level of appointment. Excellent communication, collaboration and relationship-building skills, with the ability to work effectively with students, colleagues, industry partners and the wider community.   College of Business, Creative Arts, Law, and Social Sciences The College of Business, Create Arts, Law and Social Sciences is a forward-thinking, future-focused place to work, teach and research. Our mission statement is “to embrace innovative knowledge and practice to tackle the challenges of our time, with a strategic focus on technology, health, and social impact.”   Guided by our mission statement and strategic industry collaborations, the College successfully provides contemporary, practical, and future-oriented education and research in business, commerce, sports, health care management, politics, public policy, international relations, law, and criminology.   Life at Flinders They say know your place. We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia) Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document. This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the relevant position description. Level A: Associate Lecturer (Teaching & Research) OR Associate Lecturer (Teaching Specialist) Level B: Lecturer (Teaching & Research) OR Lecturer (Teaching Specialist) Level C: Senior Lecturer (Teaching & Research) OR Senior Lecturer (Teaching Specialist) Level D: Associate Professor (Teaching & Research) OR Associate Professor (Teaching Specialist) Level E: Professor (Teaching & Research) OR Professor (Teaching Specialist) Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Vipul Pare . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 01 Dec 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    }
  },
